-- Q1: medium / L2 — Indirect (effective) ownership percentage
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Waverly Corp. owns 80% of Dalton Inc., and Dalton owns 60% of Finch Co. All three entities are consolidated. What is Waverly''s effective (indirect) ownership interest in Finch Co.?', '["60%", "80%", "48%", "40%"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 810-10, effective ownership is computed by multiplying successive ownership percentages along the chain. Waverly''s indirect interest in Finch = 80% × 60% = 48%. The 60% figure ignores the intermediate ownership link, and 80% incorrectly treats Dalton''s ownership as irrelevant. The multiplicative chain is required for multi-tier consolidation structures.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — Definition of control for consolidation
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Under ASC 810, what is the general criterion that requires a parent to consolidate a subsidiary?', '["Ownership of at least 20% of voting stock", "The parent has control over the subsidiary", "The parent owns more than 50% of the subsidiary''s debt", "The parent and subsidiary operate in the same industry"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 810-10-15, consolidation is required when one entity controls another. Control is typically evidenced by a majority voting interest (>50%), but it can also arise through variable interests (VIE model). Owning 20% creates a significant-influence presumption (equity method), not consolidation. Debt ownership and industry alignment are not consolidation triggers.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — Deferred tax on intercompany profit elimination
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Redstone Corp. (40% tax rate) sells inventory costing $300,000 to its wholly owned subsidiary for $500,000. At year-end, the subsidiary has not resold the inventory. In addition to eliminating the $200,000 intercompany profit and reducing inventory, what deferred tax adjustment should appear in the consolidation workpaper?', '["Deferred tax liability of $80,000", "No deferred tax adjustment is needed", "Deferred tax asset of $120,000", "Deferred tax asset of $80,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 740-10-25-3(e) and ASC 810-10-45, when intercompany profit is eliminated in consolidation, the related tax expense already recorded by the seller must also be deferred. The seller paid tax on the $200,000 profit, creating a $200,000 × 40% = $80,000 deferred tax asset (prepaid tax from a consolidated perspective). A DTL would be directionally wrong since tax was overpaid relative to consolidated income. The $120,000 figure applies the rate to the wrong base.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — Additional NCI purchase as equity transaction
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Granger Holdings owns 70% of Nash Corp. Granger purchases an additional 15% from outside shareholders for $600,000 when the carrying amount of that 15% NCI is $480,000. Granger retains control throughout. Under ASC 810-10-45, how does Granger account for this transaction?', '["Reduce NCI by $480,000 and reduce additional paid-in capital by $120,000", "Reduce NCI by $480,000 and recognize a $120,000 loss in earnings", "Increase goodwill by $120,000 and reduce NCI by $480,000", "Reduce NCI by $600,000 with no other adjustment"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 810-10-45-23, changes in a parent''s ownership interest while control is retained are equity transactions. The difference between consideration paid ($600,000) and the carrying amount of NCI acquired ($480,000) is a $120,000 reduction to APIC (or retained earnings if APIC is insufficient). No gain, loss, or goodwill adjustment is recognized. This preserves the principle that transactions with owners are recorded in equity.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — Preacquisition dividends
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Under the acquisition method, dividends declared by the acquiree before the acquisition date that will be paid to the acquirer after closing are treated as:', '["Dividend income of the acquirer", "A reduction in the acquirer''s retained earnings", "A reduction in the acquisition-date consideration (investment cost)", "An intercompany elimination entry"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 805-30-25, dividends declared by the acquiree before the acquisition date are considered a return of part of the purchase price, not income. They reduce the cost of the investment because the acquirer is essentially buying the net assets minus the declared dividend. Recording them as income would overstate both the investment and consolidated earnings.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — NCI share of subsidiary OCI
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Kelsey Corp. owns 75% of Pratt Inc. During the year, Pratt reports net income of $400,000 and other comprehensive income (OCI) of $120,000 from pension adjustments. In the consolidated financial statements, the noncontrolling interest is allocated which portion of Pratt''s total comprehensive income?', '["$100,000 (25% of net income only)", "$130,000 (25% of total comprehensive income)", "$120,000 (100% of OCI only)", "$100,000 plus a separate OCI line of $30,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 810-10-45-19, the NCI is allocated its proportionate share of the subsidiary''s total comprehensive income, which includes both net income and OCI. NCI share = 25% × ($400,000 + $120,000) = $130,000. Allocating only net income ignores that OCI also belongs to all shareholders. The allocation is a single comprehensive amount attributed to NCI, though income statement and OCI components may be shown separately.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: hard / L4 — Subsidiary dilutive securities in consolidated EPS
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Apex Corp. owns 80% of Lynx Inc. Lynx has 100,000 common shares outstanding and 10,000 in-the-money stock options (exercise price $20, average market price $50). Lynx''s net income is $600,000. When computing consolidated diluted EPS, how should Lynx''s dilutive options be treated?', '["Ignored because only the parent''s dilutive securities affect consolidated EPS", "Included by adding 10,000 shares to the consolidated denominator", "Included by adding the options'' intrinsic value to consolidated net income", "Included by computing Lynx''s diluted EPS, then using the diluted NCI allocation to adjust the numerator"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-30, a subsidiary''s dilutive securities must be reflected in consolidated diluted EPS. The correct approach is to first compute the subsidiary''s diluted EPS (reflecting the incremental shares from its options under the treasury stock method), then recalculate the NCI''s share using diluted amounts. This adjusts the consolidated numerator. Simply adding subsidiary options to the parent''s denominator is incorrect because subsidiary shares are not the same as parent shares.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Intercompany operating lease elimination
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Harmon Corp. leases office space to its wholly owned subsidiary under a 5-year operating lease at $120,000 per year. In the consolidated financial statements, what elimination entry is required for the current year?', '["Eliminate rent revenue, rent expense, and the subsidiary''s right-of-use asset and lease liability", "Eliminate only the subsidiary''s right-of-use asset and lease liability; the income statement items remain", "Eliminate $120,000 of rent revenue only; the expense remains as an operating cost", "No elimination is needed because the lease is at market rates"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 810-10-45, all intercompany transactions must be eliminated in consolidation regardless of whether they are at arm''s length. For an intercompany operating lease, the lessor''s rental revenue and the lessee''s rental expense are eliminated against each other. Additionally, the lessee''s right-of-use asset and lease liability (recognized under ASC 842) are eliminated because from a consolidated perspective, the entity cannot lease property to itself. Both income statement and balance sheet effects are removed.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: hard / L4 — Mutual holdings (subsidiary owns parent stock)
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Zenith Corp. owns 80% of Apex Inc. During the year, Apex purchases 5% of Zenith''s outstanding common stock on the open market for $200,000. In the consolidated financial statements, how should Apex''s investment in Zenith stock be reported?', '["As treasury stock of the consolidated entity", "As a marketable equity security at fair value", "As a long-term investment under the equity method", "As a reduction of noncontrolling interest"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 810-10-45-5, when a subsidiary holds shares of its parent, those shares are reported as treasury stock in the consolidated financial statements. From a consolidated perspective, the group has effectively reacquired its own shares. They are not treated as an investment because a single economic entity cannot hold an investment in itself. Reducing NCI or applying the equity method would be conceptually incorrect in this context.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q10: hard / L3 — Investee issues new shares causing dilution gain
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Beacon Holdings owns 40% (400,000 shares) of Spectral Corp. carried at $2,000,000 under the equity method. Spectral issues 100,000 new shares to outside investors at $8 per share when its book value is $5 per share. After the issuance, Beacon''s ownership drops to 36.4%. Under the equity method, how should Beacon account for this change?', '["No adjustment because Beacon did not sell any shares", "Recognize a gain reflecting the increase in Beacon''s share of Spectral''s net assets", "Write down the investment for the ownership dilution loss", "Reclassify the investment to fair value through net income"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 323-10-40-1, when an investee issues shares at a price above its book value per share, the investor''s proportionate share of the investee''s total net assets increases even though its ownership percentage decreases. The investor recognizes a gain equal to the change in its share of the investee''s underlying equity. Because Spectral issued shares above book value ($8 vs. $5), Beacon''s claim on net assets rises, producing a dilution gain. No adjustment only applies when shares are issued at exactly book value.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Protective vs substantive participating rights
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Venture Fund LP holds 55% of the equity of Oakmont Corp. but the limited partnership agreement grants the remaining 45% investors substantive participating rights, including approval authority over operating budgets, hiring/firing key management, and capital expenditure decisions. Under ASC 810-10-25, does Venture Fund consolidate Oakmont?', '["Yes, because Venture Fund holds majority voting control", "Yes, because 55% ownership always triggers consolidation", "Yes, but only under the VIE model", "No, because the other investors'' substantive participating rights overcome the presumption of control"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 810-10-25-13, substantive participating rights held by noncontrolling investors can overcome the presumption of control from a majority voting interest. Substantive participating rights include approval over budgets, management selection, and capital spending—all of which constrain the majority owner''s power to direct significant activities. In contrast, protective rights (e.g., veto over fundamental changes like liquidation) do not overcome the control presumption. Here, the 45% investors'' participating rights are substantive, so Venture Fund does not consolidate.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Constructive bond gain/loss amortization pattern
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Warner Corp. has bonds outstanding with a carrying amount of $1,030,000 (unamortized premium of $30,000, 3 years remaining). Its subsidiary purchases these bonds for $1,010,000. In Year 1 of the constructive retirement, the consolidated workpaper recognizes a $20,000 gain. How is this gain allocated in subsequent years?', '["The $20,000 gain reverses equally over 3 years through adjustments to interest income and interest expense", "The $20,000 gain is recognized entirely in Year 1 with no subsequent adjustment", "The gain is amortized against goodwill over the remaining bond term", "The gain is allocated entirely to the subsidiary''s interest income"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 810-10-45, a constructive retirement gain or loss is recognized in full in the consolidated statements in the year of the intercompany bond purchase. In subsequent years, the eliminating entries reverse portions of the gain as the parent''s premium amortization and the subsidiary''s discount amortization both reduce toward par. The gain effectively reverses through equal annual adjustments to consolidated interest expense and interest income over the remaining 3-year term. The gain is not charged to goodwill.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: hard / L4 — Mandatorily redeemable NCI as liability
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Sterling Group owns 70% of Jade Corp. The 30% noncontrolling shareholders have a put option requiring Sterling to repurchase their shares at fair value upon exercise. Under ASC 480-10-25, how should this noncontrolling interest be classified on the consolidated balance sheet?', '["As a component of permanent equity", "As temporary (mezzanine) equity between liabilities and equity", "As a liability measured at the redemption amount", "As a contra-equity account reducing consolidated equity"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 480-10-25-8, financial instruments that embody an unconditional obligation to repurchase equity shares by transferring assets are classified as liabilities. A put option held by NCI shareholders that is mandatorily redeemable or redeemable at the option of the holder meets this definition. The NCI is reclassified from equity to a liability measured at the redemption (fair value) amount. Mezzanine equity applies under SEC guidance (ASR 268) when redemption is outside the issuer''s control but ASC 480 does not apply; here, ASC 480 governs because of the mandatory repurchase obligation.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Multi-level consolidation with indirect NCI
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Alliance Corp. owns 80% of Baker Inc., which owns 60% of Crest Co. Crest reports net income of $1,000,000. Baker has no other operations or income. What total noncontrolling interest income is reported on Alliance''s consolidated income statement?', '["$400,000", "$520,000", "$200,000", "$120,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 810-10-45, multi-tier consolidations require NCI allocations at each level. Direct NCI in Crest = 40% × $1,000,000 = $400,000. Baker''s income (its 60% share of Crest) = $600,000. Alliance''s 20% NCI in Baker = 20% × $600,000 = $120,000. Total consolidated NCI = $400,000 + $120,000 = $520,000. The $400,000 answer ignores the indirect NCI through Baker, and the $120,000 answer captures only Baker''s NCI while ignoring Crest''s direct outside shareholders.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Purpose of consolidated financial statements
(2, 'Consolidations', 'What is the primary purpose of consolidated financial statements?', '["To present a parent and its subsidiaries as a single economic entity", "To report each subsidiary''s results on a separate line item", "To eliminate the parent company''s individual transactions", "To comply with the equity method of accounting"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 810-10-10-1, consolidated financial statements present the financial position and results of operations of a parent and its subsidiaries as if they were a single economic entity. Individual subsidiary results are combined line by line, not presented on separate line items. The equity method applies to significant-influence investments (20–50%), not to controlled subsidiaries requiring consolidation.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Loss of significant influence transition
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Kepler Corp. holds a 30% equity method investment in Orion Inc. with a carrying amount of $900,000. Kepler sells 15% of Orion for $520,000, reducing its ownership to 15% and losing significant influence. The fair value of the retained 15% interest is $500,000. Under ASC 323-10-35, what total gain or loss does Kepler recognize?', '["Gain of $20,000 on the shares sold only", "Gain of $120,000 on the shares sold plus no adjustment on the retained interest", "Loss of $80,000 on the shares sold plus a remeasurement loss on the retained interest", "Gain of $120,000 on the sold shares plus a gain on remeasurement of the retained interest"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 323-10-35-36, when an investor loses significant influence, the entire investment is affected. The 15% sold generates a gain of $520,000 − $450,000 (half of $900,000 carrying amount) = $70,000. The retained 15% is remeasured to fair value of $500,000, producing a gain of $500,000 − $450,000 = $50,000. Total gain = $120,000. Going forward, the retained 15% is accounted for at fair value under ASC 321. Both components must be recognized; ignoring the remeasurement would understate the gain.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — Equity method with investee preferred stock
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Falcon Group holds 40% of the common stock of Dove Corp. Dove reports net income of $800,000 and has $2,000,000 of 6% cumulative preferred stock outstanding held by outside investors. Under the equity method, what amount of equity income should Falcon recognize?', '["$320,000", "$272,000", "$200,000", "$280,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 323-10-35, the equity method investor''s share of income is based on the investee''s income available to common shareholders. Dove''s preferred dividend requirement = $2,000,000 × 6% = $120,000. Income available to common = $800,000 − $120,000 = $680,000. Falcon''s share = 40% × $680,000 = $272,000. Using $320,000 (40% × $800,000) ignores the preferred dividend deduction. Preferred dividends must be subtracted regardless of whether they are declared because the stock is cumulative.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Partial-year downstream sale elimination
(2, 'Consolidations', 'On September 1, Year 1, Bridgewater Corp. acquires 100% of Clover Inc. Between September 1 and December 31, Bridgewater sells $600,000 of inventory to Clover at a 25% gross margin. At year-end, 40% of this inventory remains in Clover''s warehouse. What intercompany profit must be eliminated in the Year 1 consolidated statements?', '["$150,000", "$60,000", "$100,000", "$40,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Intercompany transactions are eliminated only for the period after the acquisition date. From September 1 to December 31, Bridgewater sold $600,000 to Clover with a 25% margin, so total intercompany profit = $600,000 × 25% = $150,000. Of this, 40% remains unsold in ending inventory, so unrealized profit = $150,000 × 40% = $60,000. The $60,000 is eliminated from inventory and from consolidated income. The $150,000 answer incorrectly eliminates all profit regardless of resale status. Only unrealized profit (goods still in inventory) is eliminated.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Acquisition date definition
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Under ASC 805, the acquisition date in a business combination is defined as the date on which:', '["The acquirer obtains control of the acquiree", "The purchase agreement is signed by both parties", "The board of directors approves the transaction", "Regulatory approval is received"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 805-10-25-6, the acquisition date is the date on which the acquirer obtains control of the acquiree, which is generally the closing date when consideration is transferred and net assets or equity interests are obtained. The signing of the purchase agreement, board approval, or regulatory clearance may occur before the acquirer actually obtains control and therefore do not define the acquisition date.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Line-by-line combination
(2, 'Consolidations', 'In preparing consolidated financial statements, the individual assets, liabilities, revenues, and expenses of the subsidiary are:', '["Reported on a single line on the parent''s balance sheet and income statement", "Disclosed only in the notes to the financial statements", "Netted against the parent''s corresponding accounts", "Combined with the parent''s accounts on a line-by-line basis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 810-10-45, consolidation requires combining the parent''s and subsidiary''s financial statement line items on a line-by-line basis (e.g., subsidiary cash is added to parent cash, subsidiary revenue is added to parent revenue). A single-line presentation describes the equity method, not full consolidation. Netting is incorrect because intercompany items are eliminated, not netted against unrelated balances.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Goodwill qualitative assessment (Step 0)
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Mesa Corp. has a reporting unit with $4,000,000 of goodwill. Before performing a quantitative impairment test, Mesa''s management performs a qualitative assessment and concludes it is more likely than not that the reporting unit''s fair value exceeds its carrying amount. Under ASU 2011-08 (ASC 350-20-35), what is the next step?', '["Proceed to the quantitative test to confirm the qualitative conclusion", "No further testing is required for the current period", "Write down goodwill by 50% as a precautionary measure", "Obtain an independent third-party valuation of the reporting unit"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 350-20-35-3A (ASU 2011-08), an entity may first assess qualitative factors to determine whether it is more likely than not (>50% probability) that the reporting unit''s fair value is less than its carrying amount. If the qualitative assessment indicates fair value more likely than not exceeds carrying amount, no quantitative test is required. This ''Step 0'' option reduces cost and complexity. A quantitative test is only necessary when the qualitative assessment suggests possible impairment.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: medium / L2 — Equity method excess allocated to inventory
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Stanton Corp. pays $1,250,000 for a 25% interest in Hull Inc. Hull''s book value of net assets is $4,800,000 (Stanton''s share = $1,200,000). The $50,000 excess is attributable entirely to Hull''s undervalued inventory, which is sold to third parties within 60 days of acquisition. Hull reports net income of $800,000 for the year. What is Stanton''s equity income?', '["$150,000", "$200,000", "$250,000", "$50,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 323-10-35-13, the excess of cost over the investor''s share of book value allocated to inventory is recognized as additional cost of goods sold when the inventory is sold. Stanton''s share of Hull''s income = 25% x $800,000 = $200,000. The $50,000 inventory basis difference is charged against equity income when the inventory is sold, yielding $200,000 - $50,000 = $150,000. The $200,000 answer ignores the required inventory basis difference amortization.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Investment company scope exception
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Under ASC 946, a qualifying investment company that holds a controlling interest in an operating company is generally required to:', '["Consolidate the operating company using normal procedures", "Apply the equity method to the operating company", "Report the investment at fair value with changes in net income", "Report the investment at cost minus impairment"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 946-810-45-3, investment companies within the scope of ASC 946 are exempt from normal consolidation requirements. Instead, they report all investments—including controlled subsidiaries—at fair value with changes recognized in earnings. This scope exception recognizes that fair value measurement is more relevant to investment company stakeholders than line-by-line consolidation. The equity method and cost method do not apply to investment companies for controlled investees.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — Contingent consideration: liability vs equity
(2, 'Consolidations', 'In a business combination, Atlas Corp. agrees to pay the former owners of Beacon Inc. an additional $500,000 in cash if Beacon achieves a revenue target within two years. Under ASC 805-30-25, how is this contingent consideration classified at the acquisition date?', '["As additional goodwill, adjusted only if the target is met", "As a contingent asset of the acquirer", "As a reduction to the purchase price until the contingency is resolved", "As a liability measured at its acquisition-date fair value"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 805-30-25-5, contingent consideration in a business combination is recognized at its acquisition-date fair value as part of the consideration transferred. Because the obligation requires a future cash payment (transfer of assets), it is classified as a liability under ASC 480 and ASC 815 guidance. It is not deferred as contingent goodwill. Liability-classified contingent consideration is subsequently remeasured at fair value each period with changes recognized in earnings, not as goodwill adjustments (ASC 805-30-35-1).', 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Intercompany depreciable asset gain reversal pattern
(2, 'Consolidations', 'On January 1, Year 1, a parent sells equipment with a net book value of $400,000 to its subsidiary for $520,000. The subsidiary depreciates the equipment straight-line over 4 years with no salvage value. In the Year 3 consolidation workpaper, what is the cumulative net adjustment to consolidated depreciable assets (equipment less accumulated depreciation)?', '["Decrease net equipment by $30,000", "Decrease net equipment by $60,000", "Decrease net equipment by $120,000", "Decrease net equipment by $90,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The original intercompany gain is $120,000. Each year, $30,000 ($120,000 ÷ 4 years) of the gain is confirmed through excess depreciation. By Year 3, three years of $30,000 have been confirmed = $90,000 realized. The remaining unrealized gain = $120,000 − $90,000 = $30,000. The consolidation adjustment reduces the asset by $120,000 (to original cost basis) and reduces accumulated depreciation by $90,000 (reversing excess depreciation), for a net decrease to equipment of $30,000. The $60,000 figure incorrectly uses only two years of reversal.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — NCI on the consolidated income statement
(2, 'Consolidations', 'On the consolidated income statement, the noncontrolling interest''s share of subsidiary net income is presented:', '["As an operating expense deducted before operating income", "As a deduction from revenues in the sales section", "As a separate allocation of consolidated net income, below the net income line", "As a component of other comprehensive income"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 810-10-45-19, the consolidated income statement must attribute consolidated net income to the controlling interest and the noncontrolling interest. The NCI''s share is presented as a separate line item that allocates consolidated net income—it is not an expense, a revenue deduction, or an OCI item. This presentation shows total consolidated net income first, then the split between controlling and noncontrolling interests.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: easy / L2 — Push-down accounting election
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Whitfield Corp. acquires 100% of Sterling Inc. for $10,000,000. Sterling''s identifiable net assets have a book value of $7,000,000 and a fair value of $9,200,000. Under ASC 805-50, is Sterling required to apply push-down accounting in its separate financial statements?', '["No, push-down accounting is an election available to the acquired entity, not a requirement", "Yes, push-down accounting is mandatory for all wholly owned subsidiaries", "Yes, but only if the purchase price exceeds 150% of book value", "No, push-down accounting was eliminated by ASU 2014-17"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 805-50 (codified by ASU 2014-17), push-down accounting is an election, not a requirement. An acquired entity may choose to apply push-down accounting in its separate financial statements when a change-in-control event occurs. If elected, the subsidiary resets its assets and liabilities to the acquirer''s new basis (fair value). The election can be made at the acquisition date or subsequently if a triggering event occurs. ASU 2014-17 did not eliminate push-down—it clarified and simplified the election.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Elimination of subsidiary pre-acquisition equity
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Palmer Corp. acquires 90% of Shelton Inc. for $4,500,000. At the acquisition date, Shelton has common stock of $1,000,000, APIC of $2,000,000, and retained earnings of $1,500,000. In the acquisition-date consolidation workpaper, the elimination entry removes Shelton''s equity against:', '["Only the portion equal to Palmer''s 90% share ($4,050,000)", "The full amount of Shelton''s stockholders'' equity ($4,500,000)", "Only Shelton''s retained earnings ($1,500,000)", "Only the excess of the investment over Shelton''s book value"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 810-10-45, the consolidation elimination entry removes 100% of the subsidiary''s stockholders'' equity accounts, not just the parent''s proportionate share. All of Shelton''s equity ($1,000,000 + $2,000,000 + $1,500,000 = $4,500,000) is eliminated. The parent''s investment account is credited, and the NCI is established for the 10% outside ownership. Eliminating only 90% would leave orphaned equity balances that double-count the subsidiary''s net assets.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Equity method vs consolidation: no consolidation
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Under U.S. GAAP, an investor that holds 35% of an investee''s voting stock and exercises significant influence but does not control the investee should:', '["Consolidate the investee''s financial statements", "Report the investment at cost with no adjustments", "Use the fair value through net income method", "Use the equity method of accounting"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 323-10-15-3, when an investor has significant influence (typically 20–50% ownership) but lacks control, the equity method is required. Consolidation applies only when the investor controls the investee (generally >50% voting interest or primary beneficiary of a VIE). The cost method is not permitted for investments with significant influence under U.S. GAAP. Fair value through net income is the default for equity investments without significant influence.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: medium / L2 — Subsidiary dividends to NCI as financing activity
(2, 'Consolidations', 'Brighton Corp. owns 75% of Mercer Inc. During the year, Mercer declares and pays $400,000 in total cash dividends. On the consolidated statement of cash flows, how are the dividends paid to Mercer''s noncontrolling shareholders classified?', '["As a financing activity of $100,000", "As an operating activity of $100,000", "As a reduction of consolidated retained earnings with no cash flow effect", "As a financing activity of $400,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 230-10-45-15, dividends paid to noncontrolling shareholders are classified as financing activities on the consolidated statement of cash flows. The NCI share = 25% × $400,000 = $100,000. The parent''s 75% share ($300,000) is an intercompany transaction that is eliminated in consolidation and does not appear on the consolidated cash flow statement. Classifying the NCI dividends as operating would be incorrect since dividends to owners are financing activities. The $400,000 figure incorrectly includes the intercompany portion.', 'medium', 2)
;

COMMIT;
